    # Bridging the Implementation Gap: Intellectual Property Literacy, Perceived Institutional Readiness, and Innovation Practice among Pharmaceutical Professionals in Post-Liberation Syria

[!\[DOI](https://zenodo.org/badge/DOI/10.5281/zenodo.20716882.svg)](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20716882)
[!\[License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
[!\[Python 3.12](https://img.shields.io/badge/Python-3.12-blue.svg)](https://www.python.org/)
[!\[Target Journal](https://img.shields.io/badge/Journal-JIPR-green.svg)](https://nopr.niscpr.res.in/handle/123456789/45)

## Overview \& Executive Summary

This open-science reproducibility repository contains the complete dataset, statistical analysis plan, compliance checklists, and fully executable Python audit code for the empirical study investigating the structural relationships among **Intellectual Property Literacy (PILS)**, **Innovation Attitudes (IAI)**, **Perceived Institutional Readiness (IRS)**, and **Innovation Practice (IPI)** among pharmaceutical professionals in post-liberation Syria ($N = 303$).

### Key Empirical Findings

1. **Attitudinal Decoupling ($H\_1$ Confirmed):** High professional endorsement of IP and innovation ($\\text{IAI} = 71.30 \\pm 8.27%$) is statistically decoupled from reported innovation practice ($\\text{IPI} = 10.45 \\pm 17.91%$), yielding a massive **Diagnostic Gap of 60.84 points** ($r\_{s(\\text{IAI}, \\text{IPI})} = -0.031, p = 0.587$).
2. **System-Wide Structural Constraints ($H\_2$ Supported):** Severe floor effects were observed in operational constructs ($48.51%$ zeros in IRS, $71.95%$ zeros in IPI). Multivariable Tobit regression (left-censored at 0 via Maximum Likelihood Estimation) confirmed that individual-level knowledge, attitudes, and perceived readiness fail to statistically predict practice participation ($\\beta\_{\\text{IRS}} = -0.231, p = 0.2101$), demonstrating that practice paralysis operates at a macro-institutional level.
3. **Sectoral Floor Effect ($H\_3$ Confirmed):** Reported practice remains uniformly depressed across all professional categories (Undergraduate Students: $11.01%$, Academic Researchers: $9.88%$, Practicing Pharmacists: $6.67%$; ANOVA $F = 0.694, p = 0.5003$; Kruskal-Wallis $H = 0.996, p = 0.6078$).

